<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jugol</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jugol is the defensive wall of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It was built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid">Nur ibn Mujahid</a> in the mid-sixteenth century, after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a>. It is a lasting monument of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, and the walled city is now a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jugol.jpg" alt="Jugol" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rod Waddington from Kergunyah, Australia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_Gate,_Harar_Jugol_(14464345823).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
